# Copy to .env before `docker compose up`. This file feeds the CONTAINER;
# project/backend/.env stays dev-only.
JWT_SECRET=replace-with-a-long-random-string
# Image to pull when using compose.deploy.yml (your registry/owner/repo).
# SOFOS_IMAGE=registry.example.com/owner/sofos
# Host port the app is published on (optional, default 3000)
# SOFOS_PORT=3000
# Virus scanning (clamd, baked into the image) is ON by default (SCAN_ENABLED=true
# in the image's ENV). Uncomment to override — e.g. to disable on a box too small
# to spare the ~1.5GB clamd needs.
# SCAN_ENABLED=false
# Where account-deletion backups (export-then-purge) are written. Baked into
# the image (BACKUPS_DIR=/app/backups) and mounted as the sofos-backups
# volume — override only if you know you need a different in-container path.
# BACKUPS_DIR=/app/backups
# Where the activity log and mongod's log land (in-app 30/60/90 retention —
# see docs/DEPLOY.md "Logs and retention"). Baked into the image
# (LOGS_DIR=/app/logs) and mounted as the sofos-logs volume — override only
# if you know you need a different in-container path.
# LOGS_DIR=/app/logs
# Total upload size cap in bytes, enforced per chunk and again at finalize.
# Unset or 0 = unlimited (the default — self-hosted, no arbitrary caps).
# MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES=0
# Set to the number of reverse proxies in front of the app (usually 1) so
# req.ip reflects the real client, not the proxy — needed for per-IP login
# rate limiting to work correctly behind one. Leave unset for direct/Tailscale
# serving (the default; no proxy in front).
# TRUST_PROXY=1
# How many failed scan attempts WITH THE SCANNER CONFIRMED UP before a
# pending_scan attachment is auto-quarantined (soft-deleted, scanStatus
# "failed"). Scanner outages never count: while clamd is down the sweeper just
# re-probes on a 1/5/15/30/60-minute ladder (loud logs) and holds all files.
# SCAN_MAX_ATTEMPTS=5
# Overall timeout (ms) for a single clamd scan, once connected — guards
# against a wedged scanner hanging a finalize request or the sweeper forever.
# SCAN_TIMEOUT_MS=60000
